 # Region Growing Segmentation
 
-Also called: Region-based segmentation, Connected component analysis (in specific contexts)
+Also called: Region-based segmentation, Connected component extraction via region growth
 
 ## Overview
-Region growing segmentation is a technique to extract a connected region from a 3D volume based on pre-defined connecting criteria, used to isolate a region of interest via a mask volume [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of identifying specific anatomical or material structures within a scalar field by expanding from seed points until boundary conditions are met. It sets itself apart from pixel-based methods like the Magic Wand tool [S5, unofficial] by operating directly on volumetric connectivity, and from global optimization methods like Min-Cut [9748-graph-based-isosurface-segmentation-min-cut.md] by relying on local homogeneity criteria rather than global energy minimization.
+Region growing segmentation extracts a connected region of interest (ROI) from a 3D volume by expanding from a seed point based on pre-defined connecting criteria, such as value range similarity [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of isolating specific anatomical or material structures for subsequent volume rendering or analysis by generating a binary mask volume [S1, unofficial]. Unlike global optimization methods like [Graph-based Isosurface Segmentation (Min-Cut)](9748-graph-based-isosurface-segmentation-min-cut.md) which solve for optimal boundaries over the entire isosurface domain, region growing is a local, iterative expansion process that is computationally efficient but prone to "leaking" through weak boundaries if noise is not handled [S1, S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Restricted Region Growing](9750-restricted-region-growing.md) by lacking explicit noise-distribution-based restrictions on seed selection, making it faster but less robust against boundary leakage in noisy data [S1, S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- A 3D scalar volume $V$ of size $D_x \times D_y \times D_z$.
-- A seed point (or set of seed points) $S_{seed}$.
-- A target value range $[v_{min}, v_{max}]$ defining homogeneity [S1, unofficial].
-- A connectivity criterion (typically 6-, 18-, or 26-neighbor connectivity) [S1, unofficial].
